Although HEAs have been studied since the 1980s, the term “high entropy alloy” was only introduced in 2004 by the Taiwanese scientist Jien-Wei Yeh [ 10 ], in reference to the significant increase in mixing entropy when the number of elements in the composition was high [ 11 ]. Initially, the definition was strictly composition-based, describing alloys containing at least five principal elements, each with an atomic concentration between 5 and 35 percent [ 10 ]. However, to better capture the underlying thermodynamic principles, a modern entropy-based definition has since been adopted. This classification categorizes alloys by their configurational entropy of mixing (∆S mix ): “high-entropy” alloys are those where ∆S mix \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
